Consider the following C++ code. Select ALL statements that are true.

```cpp
int x = 4;
int y = 0;

if ((x = 2) && (y = x + 3))
    cout << x << " " << y;
else
    cout << "Fail";
```

The programmer intends to classify scores as `A` for 90–100, `B` for 80–89, `C` for 70–79, and `F` for anything below 70. Select ALL statements that correctly identify behavior or bugs in the code.

```cpp
int score;
cin >> score;

if (score > 90 && score <= 100)
    cout << "A";
else if (score > 80 && score < 90)
    cout << "B";
else if (score >= 70 && score < 80)
    cout << "C";
else
    cout << "F";
```

Consider the following C++ code. Select ALL statements that are true.

```cpp
int n = 2;

switch (n) {
    case 1:
        cout << "A";
    case 2:
        cout << "B";
    case 3:
        cout << "C";
        break;
    case 4:
        cout << "D";
    default:
        cout << "E";
}
```

Examine the code carefully. Select ALL statements that are true.

```cpp
int x = 8;
int y = 3;

if (x > 5)
    if (y > 5)
        cout << "A";
    else
        cout << "B";
else
    cout << "C";
```

Consider the condition below. Select ALL statements that are true in C++.

```cpp
int x = 50;
if (0 < x < 10)
    cout << "Inside";
else
    cout << "Outside";
```

Consider the following code. Select ALL statements that are true.

```cpp
int x = 0;
int y = 5;

if (x != 0 && ++y > 5)
    cout << "A";

if (x == 0 || ++y > 6)
    cout << y;
```

Which of the following modifications would make the shown `switch` structure invalid or cause a compile-time error? Select ALL that apply.

```cpp
int choice = 2;
switch (choice) {
    case 1:
        cout << "A";
        break;
    case 2:
        cout << "B";
        break;
    default:
        cout << "C";
}
```

Consider the following code. Select ALL statements that are true.

```cpp
int a = 9;
int b = 4;
double result = (a < b) ? a / 2.0 : a / b;
```

Analyze the following nested ternary expression. Select ALL statements that are true.

```cpp
int x = 6;
int y = 6;
int z = 3;

int result = x > y ? 10 : y > z ? 20 : x == z ? 30 : 40;
```

Consider the following code. Select ALL statements that are true.

```cpp
int x = 15;

if (x > 5)
    cout << "A";
if (x > 10)
    cout << "B";
if (x > 20)
    cout << "C";
else
    cout << "D";
```
